WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Film Courage: Can you tell us the&nbsp;
reasons why being a screenwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.200
is impossible? Glenn Gers, Screenwriter:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:13.680
Yeah, absolutely. That actually in many ways&nbsp;
I would advise no one to be a screenwriter. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:20.080
is by far one of the dumbest arts to try and be&nbsp;
in other than maybe a large-scale architecture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.160
The first thing about screenwriting is you're&nbsp;
never getting to finish the work in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.240
words you get to finish the script but it's not&nbsp;
available to an audience until it's made by other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.360
people. So basically you're creating something&nbsp;
in order to give to a bunch of other people so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.440
they'll create another thing and frankly a&nbsp;
different thing. The thing that's based in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:49.600
your script is a production and it will change in&nbsp;
many ways. So if you really are like I can totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.600
picture this movie in my head and that's why I'm&nbsp;
writing it, get over it. It will never ever be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.600
like that even in the greatest of circumstances&nbsp;
where things are better than you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.840
It will never be as you imagine. But honestly&nbsp;
I believe that's the creative process itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.080
when you imagine a script it actually comes out&nbsp;
differently than you first imagined it or what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.000
you think. The process of transforming anything&nbsp;
into a concrete work out of an abstract thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.040
changes it and honestly when a reader reads it,&nbsp;
it's going to change again because when you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.560
he wore a red tie they're going to picture&nbsp;
a different red tie that you're picturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.520
and you have to be able to accept that. You&nbsp;
have to be able to accept that the script is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:42.320
a piece of a project that you will not have any&nbsp;
control over. So that's number one is unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.600
you really like that process of doing something&nbsp;
for other people to work from, it's frustrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.160
and I know actually a lot of writers get&nbsp;
very irritated with actors and directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.120
which is too bad because they should be more&nbsp;
respectful of the other arts but they're like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.960
they didn't say that like I thought they&nbsp;
should. Like I know how that line should go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.720
and that's not how it works the line&nbsp;
goes how it goes in their mouth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.720
The actor has to be free to act the way they&nbsp;
would act it and if you are depending on them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.600
saying it exactly the way you imagined it which is&nbsp;
frankly not on the page it's not going to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:26.960
So writing (screenwriting) is more frustrating&nbsp;
than most types of writing but then not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.800
among most types of writing you have less control&nbsp;
because you don't own the copyright to your work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.280
If you're a novelist or a playwright or any&nbsp;
other kind of writer you own your work. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.080
can't...somebody may put pressure on you. You know&nbsp;
we won't publish it unless you change it but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:47.360
actually can't change it if you write a script&nbsp;
and you sell it to a company for various reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.280
having to do with the ability to unionize back in&nbsp;
the 1930's. Screenwriters gave up their right to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:03:00.640
own their creative work which means when you hire&nbsp;
on to write something they can fire you and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.800
someone else rewrite it and you cannot do anything&nbsp;
about it and that always sounds like Oh that'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.760
fine. I'll live with that. It hurts. Everyone gets&nbsp;
fired. Everyone gets rewritten. Most of my career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.560
was rewriting other people and I had to be aware&nbsp;
of the fact that somebody out there is grinding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:24.000
their teeth and cursing my name when if I do&nbsp;
well. So screenwriting is just a tough business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.880
Then you get to the fact that for most writers&nbsp;
now you're going to be working on a staff of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.200
show which means it's not even your idea. You are&nbsp;
one of a bunch of people contributing ideas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.040
the actual writer of the show, the creator, the&nbsp;
showrunner is going to be saying yes to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:49.600
no to that. That line is good, that line is&nbsp;
good, we'll put them together. You may get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.280
creative satisfaction, you may be doing grunt&nbsp;
work, you may also I mean on a feature I wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:03.680
there was an introductory scene of a character&nbsp;
that I just hated the way the producers wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.960
this character to be introduced. They thought&nbsp;
she was cute and I thought she was reprehensible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.520
and I kept saying do you really want her to say&nbsp;
that? And they're like yeah we think it's funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.560
and I was like I didn't have a choice. I had to&nbsp;
write the scene the way they asked for it or I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:25.040
would be fired or someone else would be hired to&nbsp;
replace or rewrite that scene. That's not what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.360
most people picture as being an artist. It's&nbsp;
not what most people picture as being a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.960
but I can go on. The number of reasons that&nbsp;
you shouldn't be a screenwriter are enormous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.160
The reasons that most people want to be&nbsp;
screenwriters are misleading. First of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:49.680
all they think and it's true you are very, very&nbsp;
highly paid if you are a working screenwriter. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.240
the unions in the studio&nbsp;
system, the network system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:00.000
that is a very small percentage of the&nbsp;
people who are trying to be writers. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.200
it's like saying I want to be a baseball player&nbsp;
and you're imagining I'm going to be major league.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.360
Most people are not going to be major&nbsp;
league. The question is would you stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:17.280
playing baseball then and I can't&nbsp;
imagine saying I want to be an artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.160
but only if they'll pay me first. That's&nbsp;
not how generally arts should work.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.400
Most people imagine that screenwriters have&nbsp;
a...get to come to the writer because honestly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.520
and this is a problem of the screenwriting&nbsp;
industry, everyone who teaches they're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.120
saying like It all begins with the script!&nbsp;
The script is so important and you get all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.400
these actors and directors are saying, and it's&nbsp;
true they choose a good script to work on, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.840
like we couldn't do this without the script and&nbsp;
wannabe screenwriters are like I'm so important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.440
and I promise you, you're not. They are going to&nbsp;
change your work. They are going to misinterpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:04.960
your work. that's just part of the job. You are&nbsp;
providing a beginning to someone else's creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:14.400
process and so that's a hard thing to almost every&nbsp;
screenwriter I know is looking for a creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.800
outlet. They're looking for...they're writing&nbsp;
novels on the side or plays or they're painting or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:28.480
whatever and you wouldn't think that's what&nbsp;
being a successful screenwriter is but truthfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:36.400
it is a job. It's a glorious job and when it&nbsp;
works right you have these amazing experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.200
where talented people take your work and they&nbsp;
like your arc and they do stuff and it works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.520
and then it goes out to a big audience and&nbsp;
that's a little tiny slice of the experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:54.640
The things i've had made were amazing. They're&nbsp;
only two-three percent of what I actually worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:59.280
on. Most of what I've worked on no one will&nbsp;
ever hear of. It's trapped in some company's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.880
vault somewhere or in my drawer because no&nbsp;
one bought it and I'm talented, I'm good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.480
I'm successful, I'm in the union, I'm highly&nbsp;
paid and most of what I write is rejected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:17.680
So yeah be a painter if you can because at least&nbsp;
you get done, you have the finished painting.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.080
But the flip side of that, so that I don't&nbsp;
just discourage the heck out of everybody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:32.560
the flip side is if you get good at it you learn&nbsp;
certain skills about narrative and character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.720
that are invaluable no matter what else you try&nbsp;
to do creatively. If you want to be a director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.560
it's great to know what screenwriting really&nbsp;
is, what a scene and a character really are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:52.320
It's very important to understand how much the&nbsp;
character's motivation, the character's goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.640
the thing they're trying to do that's crucial and&nbsp;
if you're writing a novel it's just as important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.640
and if you're writing a graphic novel or&nbsp;
a video game whatever it is, those basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.240
ideas about a character, wanting something&nbsp;
and having a difficult time getting it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:16.320
that is the center of any kind of narrative&nbsp;
creativity and screenwriting pushes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.120
harder than most if you can learn it. In&nbsp;
screenwriting you can do it anywhere. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.400
it's sort of good to learn to be a screenwriter&nbsp;
because then you've got a skill set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.520
if you decide to work in video games great!&nbsp;
You're going to be really, really set.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.520
Film Courage: And when you were temping in New&nbsp;
York City, were you attempting to be an author or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.400
it was when you wrote that play and then you&nbsp;
saw it was sort of goldman-esque and you thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.320
let's do screenwriting? Glenn: No ever since I&nbsp;
was like a kid when I see something I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:54.000
do it like paint. I was painting for a while. I&nbsp;
was drawing. I was writing plays. I was trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.760
to direct movies. I was trying to write movies on&nbsp;
Super 8 silent film which means I had to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:06.240
learn how to make silent movies so I like to say&nbsp;
I came up in silent film. It was in the 1970's but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.760
that was all they had then if you were going to&nbsp;
make if you're a high school student you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:16.720
make a movie they had eight millimeter home&nbsp;
movie cameras. I would make silent movies I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:24.000
tried to write novels because i love novels, plays&nbsp;
because I love plays. I have a dangerous desire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.040
do absolutely anything I see. So no I was always&nbsp;
doing all of it and I would sort of shift my focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.920
depending on what was practical. When I was in&nbsp;
high school I got tired of silent movies. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.520
was frustrating to not have any dialogue but high&nbsp;
school they had plays so I started to write plays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.680
High school and college I did plays. I got out of&nbsp;
college I was sort of thinking of writing plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.480
sort of thinking of writing novels, but&nbsp;
screenwriting I thought that would pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:58.240
for all of them. If I could get screenwriting jobs&nbsp;
I could afford to do everything else and then for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:04.480
12 years I was an office temp and couldn't afford&nbsp;
to do anything except write in my off time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:10.960
the theory always was I was going to do it all and&nbsp;
screenwriting was just the most lucrative which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:17.600
is what everybody thinks and you know yeah for the&nbsp;
major leagues, it's very lucrative for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:31.840
us, it's not and you should really want to do it.&nbsp;
You should love doing it even if no one pays you.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:40.240
Questions For The Viewers: How do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.296
you process the information shared in this video?

